Hi,

This came up in a working session and nobody had the same definition.

The board keeps using active accounts as a proxy for customer primacy, and th=
at assumption needs to be challenged.

I do not want a dashboard that simply counts the outcome. Start with the comp=
eting explanations: fee or service friction precedes the drop in activity; re=
gular inflows and recurring payments indicate a primary relationship; or cust=
omers keep the account open but move useful activity elsewhere.

The discussion is currently being driven by anecdotes, and the teams involved=
 are describing the same customers differently. This has returned because the=
 previous answer was useful but not strong enough to become a standing rule. =
Use February 2025 as the new evidence point rather than recycling the earlier=
 conclusion.=20

What we need to decide is how Keystone should define a primary customer and w=
hich customers need service recovery rather than marketing. Please bring a mo=
del-ready table, data dictionary and an honest baseline, including a few case=
s we can trace from source to conclusion.

The available evidence is monthly account snapshots, status events, limits, p=
roduct enrolments and signatories; transaction-level timestamps, channels, am=
ounts, statuses and error context; debit-order mandates, collection dates, su=
spensions and cancellations; customer contacts, complaints and suggestions. U=
se transaction behaviour as a proxy. Do not claim to observe activity held at=
 another bank.

Thank you
